      "side": 1,
      "message": "It will be hard to maintain all these changes to the .c files, and I cannot upstream these changes as there is no justification for them upstream.",
      "revId": "d3c807f0b82485809e4d59e06d136765eb52bd2f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b3855457_b8741de1",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1956160
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-05T10:49:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "@gregkh@google.com what do you think about possibility of upstreaming this version? I understand that KMI may be not enough justification, but I have more:\n* This change makes these structures private to fs/crypto and fs/verity implementation, sending clear message that they shouldn\u0027t be accessed outside.\n* Pattern of assigning internal structure pointer to a variable than using it is more frequent through fs/crypto and fs/verity code.",
      "parentUuid": "0304b20f_8b7fb0a7",
      "revId": "d3c807f0b82485809e4d59e06d136765eb52bd2f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fbdc7f46_a0b39f93",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-05T12:37:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The better way to do this is to put the definitions of these structures ONLY in a local directory to the crypto code, and have an \"empty\" definition of the type out in the normal .h files.\n\nThat way no hook or external driver can ever get access to them, and if we \"leak\" the structure types into the abi definitions, it\u0027s ok we can update them safely as we \"know\" that no external code can ever touch them.\n\nAnd that\u0027s the best way to do it upstream as well, hide the structure type so that no other code can touch it.  I make changes like this all the time to other subsystems, most recently, the driver core.",
      "parentUuid": "b3855457_b8741de1",
      "revId": "d3c807f0b82485809e4d59e06d136765eb52bd2f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "274b4ae5_d8fd728c",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1956160
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-05T13:04:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Your suggestion is already implemented, the definitions of these structures is already local to the crypto code, and we have an forward-declaration definition of these out in the normal .h files.\n\nThe problem is that we don\u0027t have tooling that can say \"it\u0027s ok to update them, we \"know\" that no external code can ever touch them\". And approving these changes manually while bypassing presubmits frustrates people.\n\nMy solution is a workaround on these issues and I would like to understand, is this workaround acceptable and justifiable upstream? Or we have to keep these structures in KMI until we develop such tool and policy to allow changes for private structures?",
      "parentUuid": "fbdc7f46_a0b39f93",
      "revId": "d3c807f0b82485809e4d59e06d136765eb52bd2f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "907c51f2_5e184353",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-05T13:29:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah, I missed that.  Then no, all is good, there\u0027s no real need for this change at all.\n\nYes, we don\u0027t have tooling for \"it\u0027s ok to touch them\" type of stuff, that\u0027s just tribal knowledge we are getting over time here.  Now with the DDK logic preventing out-of-tree code from using internal .h files, it should be much better.\n\nSo no, this whole thing shouldn\u0027t be needed at all, if we have \"issues\" come up in the future, it will be safe to update those structures.  We do this occasionally when other things change, manual review is what is required for now, sorry.",
      "parentUuid": "274b4ae5_d8fd728c",
      "revId": "d3c807f0b82485809e4d59e06d136765eb52bd2f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    }
